        'After a project, more always changes than just your measure: volume, product mix, season, new people, another project. A factor like that, which also affects the result, is called a confounder.\n\nSo the question at the end is not “is it better?” but “how much better than it would have been without us?” You can never know that for sure, but you can estimate it reasonably.',
        'The strongest simple method is difference-in-differences. Work out the change on your line, and the change on a comparable line without the project. The difference between the two is your estimated effect.\n\nEconomists made the method well known; a classic example is Card and Krueger’s 1994 study of the minimum wage in fast-food restaurants in two US states. For a line or a region it works the same way.',
        'The method rests on one assumption: parallel trends. Without the project, both lines would have moved roughly the same way. Check that in the baseline: did the two run charts move up and down together before the project?\n\nIf the comparison line was already moving very differently, it is not a good comparison. Pick another one, or say honestly that the effect is uncertain.',
        'If volume changed, start normalising: express results per unit, such as scrap per 1,000 pieces or breakdowns per 100 running hours. More production gives more scrap in pieces, even if the process got better.\n\nThen the Hawthorne effect: people work better just because they get attention. The idea comes from experiments at Western Electric in the 1920s; Levitt and List (2011) did not find the famous pattern in the original data. Still, some attention effect is plausible, so also measure once the attention is gone.',
